MANDATORY FUNCTIONAL TESTING & ACCEPTANCE VERIFICATION

Every game feature, component, system, and interactive element must undergo actual functional testing and pass clearly defined acceptance criteria before it can be declared complete.

This applies to all game content and systems, including maps, cities, buildings, characters, NPCs, monsters, bosses, movement, navigation, combat, skills, weapons, armor, equipment, items, inventory, merchants, local buying and selling transactions, quests, rewards, character progression, saving and loading, and all interconnected offline gameplay mechanics.

For every feature or system:

1. Implement Before Testing: Complete its actual functionality, gameplay logic, interactions, dependencies, and required integrations before considering it ready for acceptance testing.

2. Execute Real Tests: Launch and operate the actual implementation. Test its real behavior through gameplay or automated tests, not through screenshots, visual inspection, simulated success messages, mock data, or assumptions.

3. Verify All Interactions: Test normal operations, user interactions, input handling, state changes, expected outputs, edge cases, failure conditions, and interactions with dependent systems.

4. Verify System Integration: Confirm that each feature works correctly with related game systems, shared local data, character states, world interactions, inventory, economy, quests, progression, and all applicable dependencies. Verify that the complete offline gameplay loop works without external multiplayer or server dependencies.

5. Verify Android Compatibility: Test gameplay functionality on the target Android device, including controls, interface, performance, memory usage, storage, installation, resource handling, and offline operation. Emulator tests may support development but do not replace actual-device verification whenever device testing is available.

6. Record Actual Results: Maintain a detailed, persistent testing and acceptance report for every feature, including:
   
   - Feature and system name.
   - Implemented functionality.
   - Tests performed and their exact procedures.
   - Expected results.
   - Actual observed results.
   - Pass/fail status for each test.
   - Identified bugs, missing functionality, and dependencies.
   - Evidence of execution, such as test logs, runtime output, screenshots, or recordings where appropriate.
   - Remaining work and the next required action.

7. Enforce Strict Completion Criteria: A feature may only be marked COMPLETE when all its required functionality has been implemented, all mandatory acceptance tests have passed, all critical dependencies are working, and its results have been recorded. Partial implementations must be marked PARTIALLY COMPLETE. Failed tests must be marked FAILED. Untested features must be marked NOT TESTED. Visual placeholders, mock implementations, mocked API responses, and simulated interactions must never qualify as completed functionality.

8. Fix and Retest: When a test fails, investigate and fix the underlying cause, then rerun the failed tests and all relevant regression tests. Do not hide, ignore, or downgrade failures to obtain a passing status.

9. Maintain a Live Progress and Test Registry: Keep an up-to-date record of every feature, its implementation status, test coverage, acceptance results, known issues, and dependencies. Preserve this registry across development sessions and resume from the actual recorded stopping point without unnecessarily repeating completed work.

10. Final Completion Audit: Before declaring the game or any major development milestone complete, perform an end-to-end acceptance audit of all implemented systems and their interactions. Verify that the game operates as a coherent, fully playable, offline MMORPG-inspired world rather than a collection of isolated features or visual demonstrations.

Non-Negotiable Rule: Never claim that a feature, system, milestone, or the game itself is complete merely because its code has been written, its interface has been created, or its visual representation looks finished. Completion must be demonstrated through successful execution, verified functionality, passing acceptance tests, and documented results. Always report the actual development and testing status honestly.

Deliver the updated existing project, final APK, resource manifest, reference research specification, development log, and an honest implementation and testing report. Clearly distinguish implemented and verified features from partial, missing, untested, or blocked features. Never claim a build, upload, deployment, download, installation, or test succeeded without actual evidence.

FINAL OBJECTIVE

Transform the existing CLASH OF LEGENDS project into a complete, visually improved, independent Android MMORPG-inspired personal game, using Conquer Online Mobile as its exclusive gameplay reference. Preserve existing progress, research and implement the complete connected gameplay systems, package the largest practical portion of resources into one APK, and automatically download and install only the small essential remainder through the game's own interface after the user's approval on first launch. Resume development from the last verified checkpoint until the actual deliverables and test results are ready.

